#limitation(title: [The Mast-Cell "Energy-Envelope Drain" Is an Untested Hypothesis, Not a Finding])[
The proposition that chronic mast-cell degranulation is a measurable energy drain — an ATP/Ca²⁺ cost of repeated exocytosis that shrinks the patient's energy envelope — has no supporting literature. A search for "mast cell degranulation energy metabolism fatigue" returns zero directly relevant hits. The closest evidence is histamine-specific and does not measure mast-cell metabolic cost: histamine favours endurance and protects against exhaustion in histidine-decarboxylase-knockout mice @NiijimaYaoita2012HistamineFatigue, while histamine-receptor blockade leaves the exercise domain boundary and time-to-task-failure unchanged in healthy adults @Abbotts2025HistamineExercise. Neither study measures the ATP or Ca²⁺ burden of degranulation itself, so the energy-drain claim is a plausible-but-unmeasured hypothesis, not an established mechanism, and must not be stated as if it were measured.

*Certainty:* N/A (no evidence exists to assign a probability; the claim is unquantifiable as stated).

*Consequence:* The energy-envelope drain should be presented strictly as an untested hypothesis to be investigated (for example, by measuring the ATP/Ca²⁺ cost of IgE-mediated degranulation in isolated human mast cells), never as an established contributor to ME/CFS fatigue, because doing so would overstate the evidence base.
] <lim:mast-cell-energy-envelope-drain-untested>
